<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huaca del Sol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 100–<a href="/years/800">800 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Temple of the Sun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huaca del Sol, 'Temple of the Sun', is a vast stepped platform of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud brick</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trujillo-peru">Trujillo, Peru</a>, built by the Moche culture between about 100 and 800 CE. Made of an estimated hundred million adobe bricks, many bearing makers' marks, it was one of the largest adobe structures in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and probably a political and administrative centre. In the 17th century Spanish treasure hunters diverted a river to wash away part of it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huaca-del-sol.jpg" alt="Huaca del Sol" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huaca_Sol_lou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
